WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.460
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Four things I thought I would
touch on, the first being the performance

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.759
of the National Health Service over the last
several months. We will have a detailed discussion

00:00:22.759 --> 00:00:28.320
on this a bit later in the agenda, led by
Matthew and Karen, but it happens that our

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.890
meeting today coincides with the 09.30 release
of the monthly performance stats, which is

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:39.240
the reason our Board papers went up online
after 09.30.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.280
What they show, of course, is what we all
know, which is, there has been great pressure

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.140
across the National Health Service, across
hospitals, over the November/December period.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:56.680
That has carried on into January and February,
so I think the first thing to acknowledge

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:04.510
is that those pressures are real, that staff
are working under very difficult circumstances,

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:11.230
and we should I think collectively record
our great thanks and appreciation for what

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:17.190
everybody is doing. I think that has been
very visible to the people of this country,

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:22.050
not least through programmes such as the BBC
Hospital programme, which has shown some of

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:29.810
the real pressures that are out there. Emergency
medical admissions were up 2.1% in December

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:35.680
this year compared with a year ago, and we
really have two things going on here, which

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:42.140
our delivery plan that we publish at the end
of March will have to firmly address. At the

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:47.610
front end, it is the increase in the number
of A&amp;E attendances, and part of the answer

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:56.300
to that, of course, has to be a streamlined
urgent treatment centre offer, extended access

00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:03.860
to convenient primary care, and substantial
changes to the way 111 works and is networked

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:14.170
with other urgent care services. Really, the
principal driver of the backing-up of major

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:20.319
medical admissions and delays in A&amp;E itself
is the availability of inpatient beds in the

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:26.520
hospital that people are able to admit to,
and the single biggest change over the course

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:32.620
of the last year, two years and three years,
has been the effective reduction in the bed

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:37.760
availability as a result of the fact that
social care-related delayed discharges have

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.520
gone up by 90% over the course of the last
two years. I think this is now widely understood.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:48.520
I think people are mobilising to get a solution;
there are discussions underway, not only with

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:52.640
local government, but with central government,
about that, and that is obviously going to

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.500
be an important part of the solution, going
forward.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.720
I think just to mention two other related
aspects on performance though, which are perhaps

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:08.810
not so widely appreciated. GPs are successfully
acting to moderate demand growth for elective

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:18.970
services, and the three-month referral growth
rate of just 1.5% is the lowest referral growth

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:25.370
rate we have seen in a number of years now.
The referral growth rate is two thirds down

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:34.090
on what it was last year. At the same time,
elective activity is accelerating as the year

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:42.440
proceeds, so we performed more NHS-funded
operations in the period October, November

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:48.800
and December than in April, May and June,
and that was more, of course, than the year

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.710
before, as well. As we will get onto, I think
there are a lot of pressures out there, and

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:58.739
we can discuss in more detail what those look
like.

00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:04.479
Second point to report is the pleasing news
that we have reached agreement with the GP

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.300
Committee of the BMA on the GP contract for
next year, and I think this deals with a number

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:19.049
of the legitimate pressures and concerns that
GPs have, while also ensuring that some of

00:04:19.049 --> 00:04:28.460
our wider objectives are met, including ensuring
that practices are incentivised not to shut

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:34.010
for half-days on a weekly basis, the small
proportion of practices that do that. We recognise

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:39.760
this is just one piece of a much bigger jigsaw
of improvement that is needed in primary care,

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:46.370
but I do think it represents another concrete
step in the turnaround in investment, and

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.530
in changed working practices and services
in general practice, and it is pleasing it

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:56.840
has been welcomed by GPs, as well as by patients
groups.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.750
Two topical things that have arisen in respect
to general practice, however, that it is worth

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:09.590
just noting. There have been reports this
week of a practice in Dorset proposing to

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:14.930
start offering private charged-for appointments
alongside its NHS appointments. I think we

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:22.360
want to be absolutely clear that it is not
permissible for a practice to charge its own

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:29.630
registered patients, and even the circumstances
that are being described here look questionable,

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:34.389
so we are going to take a very, very hard
look at NHS England at anybody proposing that

00:05:34.389 --> 00:05:43.900
kind of approach. Likewise, there was a suggestion
that individual CCGs might choose to change

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:49.581
the national contract in respect of QOF at
the back-end of this year in Leeds. Legal

00:05:49.581 --> 00:05:54.930
advice is clear that that would not be possible
and in fact is not the approach that we are

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:59.490
going to be taking, given that a lot of practices
have said to us that, ten months into the

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:05.380
year, messing around with the QOF payments
would be very unfair and would not actually

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:12.180
create a lot of extra headroom for the other
pressures that they are facing.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:19.030
Thirdly, just to report that we have various
continuing conversations with local authorities.

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:27.000
I have been discussing with the Mayor of London,
Sadiq Khan, what the next phase of the London

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:34.150
devolution agreement looks like, and we expect
to be able to make further announcements on

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:41.880
that shortly. Also, conversations with the
West Midlands authorities who have been doing

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:48.920
work on health and work and mental health,
led by Norman Lamb. Then a number of other

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:53.210
local authorities around the country as part
of the STP implementation process are in discussion

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:56.289
with us about the role that they might play.

00:06:56.289 --> 00:07:02.560
That brings me on fourthly to the Five Year
Forward View delivery plan, which we are going

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:07.979
to be publishing at the end of March. As you
recall, the legal status of this document

00:07:07.979 --> 00:07:13.229
is, it is our NHS England business plan, but
rather than that just being for the year ahead,

00:07:13.229 --> 00:07:18.229
this will look out for the next two years,
and for some of the deliverables, a bit beyond

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:25.741
that. It will be a shared effort with other
national leadership bodies in the NHS. It

00:07:25.741 --> 00:07:33.910
is not a bidding document for NHS funding;
instead, what it is, is taking as the starting

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:41.259
point funding that the government has allocated
to the NHS for 2017 and 2018, and then within

00:07:41.259 --> 00:07:48.740
that, saying, ‘What realistically can the
NHS deliver over that period, both against

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:54.479
the usual measures that people are interested
in, but particularly on the key priorities

00:07:54.479 --> 00:07:59.919
that we have previously set out, so on mental
health, on cancer, on the other national goals

00:07:59.919 --> 00:08:05.349
that this organisation has been advancing
for several years ago, with constancy of purpose,

00:08:05.349 --> 00:08:10.050
what in practice can be done over the next
two years?’

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:16.940
It will also signal very clearly that STPs
are here to stay, and a number of us had a

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:27.310
very productive session with the 44 STP leads
and NHS Improvement last week. Coming out

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:33.050
of that, I think we will be in the delivery
plan clearly signalling that we will be moving

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:40.939
towards a formal appointment process for STP
leads across the country, bringing together

00:08:40.939 --> 00:08:48.050
resources that exist in NHS England, together
with CCG resources, trust resources, to beef

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:54.750
up the patient capability that exists at STP
level, giving them decision rights not only

00:08:54.750 --> 00:09:02.489
over many of our activities, but also the
ability to recommend changes to the configuration

00:09:02.489 --> 00:09:09.899
or governance of constituent statutory organisations
in those geographies where the STP believe

00:09:09.899 --> 00:09:15.980
that veto power or inertia is getting in the
way of the bigger strategic change agenda

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:23.850
that is required in that part of the country.
A lot of work going on now around this delivery

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:31.230
plan, I think responding to the request from
the NHS about realism, about the pressures

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:39.160
we are facing, clarity about the priorities,
and linking that clearly to a new way of working

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:41.999
that brings all of the pieces together to
get the job done.

00:09:41.999 --> 00:09:49.929
Malcolm Grant: Thank you. I have a proposal
to put to the Board. We had a very extensive

00:09:49.929 --> 00:09:56.680
run informally yesterday around the delivery
plan. I think we have managed to agree on

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:04.829
a number of key concepts, tone and ideas that
should go into it. It is also, as Simon said,

00:10:04.829 --> 00:10:10.709
our business plan, and that is a matter which
is reserved to the Board to approve. However,

00:10:10.709 --> 00:10:15.980
it is clear that in the normal schedule of
our meetings, we would be anxious for the

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:22.700
publication of the paper to take place before
our next Board meeting on 30th March, so what

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:28.730
I would propose to the Board is that you authorise
delegation of final approval to the Chief

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:33.899
Executive and the Chair and the Deputy Chair
together, but that a condition of that is

00:10:33.899 --> 00:10:39.860
that we will, throughout the process, between
now and then, have much further iteration

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:44.699
between all members of the Board, and I am
proposing that we should bring together certainly

00:10:44.699 --> 00:10:52.739
the non-executives at a point before publication,
so that we have a final opportunity to go

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:57.480
through the draft report, so that it will
not go out to publication until every member

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:02.019
of the Board is content with it. I would ask
you to approve that delegation so that we

00:11:02.019 --> 00:11:06.879
have a formal governance structure in place
to ensure that we have a proper, deliverable

00:11:06.879 --> 00:11:10.399
product.
Are members of the Board content with that?

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:12.100
Okay, thank you very much.

